  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Given that $3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)$\n- $4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$",
  "worked_solution": "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a & ≡3b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ 3\\cdot a & ≡3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 3) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement I and the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟺\\,4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
